Subject: [PATCH net 0/2] forcedeth: two register-window bounds fixes

Two bounds fixes in forcedeth, both in the same shape: a loop that walks
the register window one step too far. They are independent of each other
and touch different functions.

1/2 nv_suspend() and nv_resume() save and restore the non-PCI config
    space with i <= register_size/sizeof(u32). On a VER3 device that is
    exactly the length of saved_config_space[], so the last iteration
    writes one element past the array on suspend, and on resume writel()s
    that value one dword past the ioremap window. UBSAN catches it.

2/2 nv_tx_timeout() dumps the window in rows of eight dwords but only
    bounds the row's starting offset, so the final row always reads
    between 12 and 28 bytes past the end of the mapping, on every one of
    the three supported window sizes.

Both are ancient: 1/2 dates to 2008, 2/2 to 2006. Neither is a
regression, and neither has ever been reported as far as I can tell,
which is unsurprising given the next section.

Why these matter more than "reads a bit too far"

  np->base = ioremap(addr, np->register_size) maps exactly
  register_size bytes, so every overrun above leaves the mapping rather
  than merely running past a notional register range. On x86 the ioremap
  is rounded up to page granularity, so nothing faults in practice. That
  is presumably why both survived this long, and it is also why I would
  not describe either as urgent.

Testing

  Reference hardware: Apple Mac mini Late 2009, MCP79 chipset, forcedeth
  driving the onboard NIC.

  1/2 is reproduced and fixed on hardware. With the stock module, one
  deep S3 cycle produces two UBSAN splats, one per loop. With the patched
  module built and loaded (md5 of the running module checked against the
  installed one), the same cycle produces none, the link comes back, the
  DHCP lease is restored and ping shows no loss. Most recently confirmed
  on 7.1.8, where a single S3 cycle reported :6225 from pci_pm_suspend
  and :6240 from pci_pm_resume.

  2/2 is arithmetic and a compile test only. Its code path sits behind
  the debug_tx_timeout module parameter and needs a genuine TX timeout to
  run, which I cannot force safely on this machine. I would rather say
  that plainly than imply a test I did not do.

Two checkpatch notes, both deliberate

  1/2 trips "Prefer a maximum 75 chars per line" on a line that is the
  verbatim UBSAN splat. I left the quote intact rather than rewrap
  diagnostic output to satisfy a heuristic.

  1/2 also trips two "spaces preferred around that '/'" CHECKs on
  register_size/sizeof(u32). That spelling is what the file already uses,
  including in the untouched identical loop in nv_get_regs(). Adding
  spaces would leave the two lines I touch inconsistent with their own
  neighbourhood, so I kept the fix minimal. Happy to change it if you
  prefer the other way round.

